<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We Can Swim. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> We Can Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose messages to invite peers to a performance, event, or activity. Interactive: Exchange messages, letters, or texts with classmates or peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose an invitation to a sports event (e.g., "Come to our sports day!"). Can exchange details in a message (e.g., "The sports day is on Friday at 10 a.m."). Can exchange simple messages about activities and events (e.g., "Let's take a walk!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various sports and activities (e.g., swimming, football, cycling, running, hiking, dancing). The teacher asks students, "What sport do you like?" and encourages them to write their answer on a mini-whiteboard or a small piece of paper. Students show their written answers (e.g., "I like swimming.", "I like football."). Teacher points to words like "can" and "swim" and explicitly models the pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple invitation on the board: "Hi Alex! Let's go swimming on Friday at 3 p.m. at the gym. It's fun! See you! Maria." The teacher reads it aloud, pointing to each part. Teacher models how to ask about preferences: "Do you like to swim, Alex?" and expresses preference: "Yes, I do. I like swimming." while writing these sentences. The teacher also demonstrates segmenting "exciting" as "ex-cit-ing" and "wellness" as "well-ness" when discussing healthy activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a long letter or a short message?" (Expected answer: "A short message.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What day is the swimming activity?" (Expected answer: "Friday.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we invite a friend with this message?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents two different simple written invitation messages projected on the board or on a handout. One invites to swimming, and the other to cycling. </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; border: 1px dashed #aaa; padding: 5px; background-color: #f9f9f9;">
                <b>Invitation 1:</b><br>
                Hi Leo!<br>
                Let's go swimming at the pool on Saturday at 10 a.m. It's an exciting activity! We can swim together! You can do it!<br>
                See you!<br>
                Sara
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; border: 1px dashed #aaa; padding: 5px; background-color: #f9f9f9;">
                <b>Invitation 2:</b><br>
                Dear Ana,<br>
                Do you like cycling? I like cycling every day! Let's ride our bikes this afternoon in the park. We can be active and healthy!<br>
                Your friend,<br>
                Tom
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the main information and purpose of short, simple written invitations to activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the invitations. Teacher asks students to *write* the answers to the following questions:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which invitation is for swimming? (Write 1 or 2)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who invites Leo? (Write the name)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What time is the cycling? (Write the time)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What type of exercise does Tom like? (Write the answer)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences and short messages using modal verbs (can/cannot), present simple questions and affirmative sentences (e.g., "Do you like sports?", "I ride my bike every day."), WH-questions + answer + subject + verb + ing (e.g., "What type of exercise do you like? I like swimming."), and the target vocabulary (e.g., swim, play, run, go, friend, gym, afternoon, exciting, healthy, active, every day).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> The teacher provides a partially completed invitation template. Students complete the blanks using words from a provided word bank (e.g., 'friend', 'swimming', 'Friday', 'gym', 'fun', 'can').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example Template:</b><br>
                    Dear _____,<br>
                    Let's go ______ on ______ at 4 p.m. at the ______. We ______ play together! It's very _____.<br>
                    Your _____,<br>
                    [Your name]
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Ordering):</b> Students receive jumbled words or phrases related to inviting someone to an activity. In pairs, they *write* the words in the correct order to form complete sentences.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Examples:</b><br>
                    a) like / do / you / football / ? (Write: Do you like football?)<br>
                    b) cycling / go / let's / day / every (Write: Let's go cycling every day.)<br>
                    c) is / active / so / running (Write: Running is so active!)
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Writing & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three sentences about an activity they like, using different target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "I like hiking. We can hike on Saturday. Hiking is healthy!"). They then exchange their written sentences with their partner, who checks for correct spelling, grammar, and use of target vocabulary. They can suggest edits, such as adding "You can do it!" or "Let's run together!".</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product (Personalized Invitation Message)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will individually compose a short invitation message to a classmate or a friend, inviting them to a specific sports activity or exercise (e.g., swimming, football, dancing, running). They must include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A greeting (e.g., "Hi [Friend's Name]!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity (e.g., "Let's go swimming!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When and where (e.g., "on Saturday at 9 a.m. at the park.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An encouraging phrase or a reason (e.g., "It's healthy and fun!", "We can run together!", "You can do it!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A closing (e.g., "See you!", "Your friend, [Your Name]")</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students should focus on using the target grammar (modal verbs, present simple, WH-questions if asking for preference within the invitation) and vocabulary, aiming for clear communication. They can use a simple template provided by the teacher on a small 'invitation card' handout or write it in their notebooks. The teacher circulates, offering support and feedback on vocabulary and sentence structure while students are writing.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation and a simple rubric for assessing the written invitation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written invitation messages from Stage 4. The teacher will review them for understanding and application of the target writing skills.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the invitation message (easy to understand the activity, time, and place).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., swimming, football, friend, healthy, exciting).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "Let's go..." or "We can..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Proper sentence structure for simple invitations.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of an encouraging phrase (e.g., "You can do it!").</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new word I used in my invitation was: ________.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write an invitation to: (Circle one) a friend / my teacher / my family.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Connecting today's learning to future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for me when writing my invitation?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult, and what can I do next time to make it easier?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of writing invitations did students grasp easily? Were there common difficulties with grammar (e.g., modal verbs, present simple questions) or vocabulary usage? Were the activities engaging and appropriate for 5th grade and A1.3 level? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, specific vocabulary review) might be needed for students who struggled with composing their messages independently? How can the peer-checking activity be enhanced?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize information about sports activities and create a collaborative poster promoting a 'Sports Day' for the school, using their acquired writing skills to explain and invite peers.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>